  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 5,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 0,
          "bbox": [
            0.647,
            0.042,
            0.848,
            0.058
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Journey Inside the Atom"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "header",
          "index": 1,
          "bbox": [
            0.857,
            0.018,
            0.952,
            0.088
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "N 145"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 2,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.088,
            0.695,
            0.156
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Rutherford’s atomic model was better than Thomson’s atomic model in explaining the results of the gold foil experiment. However, it had a limitation. It could not explain the stability of the atom."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 3,
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.165,
            0.498,
            0.183
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "B. Limitations of Rutherford’s model"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 4,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.186,
            0.693,
            0.222
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "While the idea of a central nucleus was a major step forward, Rutherford’s model could not explain why atoms are stable."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 5,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.224,
            0.695,
            0.326
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "As you have learnt in Chapter 4, Describing Motion Around Us, a particle moving in a circular path is constantly changing direction, which means it is accelerating. If a negatively charged electron keeps accelerating around the nucleus, it should lose energy. Losing energy would make it spiral inward and eventually fall into the positively charged nucleus (Fig. 8.6). If that really"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 6,
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.332,
            0.351,
            0.494
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 6,
              "bbox": [
                0.113,
                0.332,
                0.351,
                0.494
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_5_image_6.jpg",
              "content": ""
            },
            {
              "type": "image_caption",
              "index": 7,
              "bbox": [
                0.11,
                0.497,
                0.351,
                0.528
              ],
              "content": [
                {
                  "type": "text",
                  "content": "Fig. 8.6: Spiral path followed by a charged particle on losing energy"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 8,
          "bbox": [
            0.37,
            0.325,
            0.695,
            0.478
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "happened, atoms would collapse and would not exist! But in reality, atoms are stable that is why matter around us stays intact. This meant that Rutherford’s model was not completely correct, and a new explanation was needed to describe how electrons remain in motion without collapsing into the nucleus."
            }
          ],
          "continues_prev": true
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 9,
          "bbox": [
            0.371,
            0.48,
            0.776,
            0.532
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "But before we come to that, you need to know about another subatomic particle, the proton, discovered and named by Rutherford."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 10,
          "bbox": [
            0.725,
            0.093,
            0.893,
            0.11
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Meet a Scientist",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 11,
          "bbox": [
            0.724,
            0.113,
            0.84,
            0.223
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 11,
              "bbox": [
                0.724,
                0.113,
                0.84,
                0.223
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_5_image_11.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 12,
          "bbox": [
            0.843,
            0.121,
            0.945,
            0.228
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Born in New Zealand, "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Ernest Rutherford",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " moved to Cambridge"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 13,
          "bbox": [
            0.723,
            0.228,
            0.942,
            0.458
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "to work with J. J. Thomson and later became known as the Father of Nuclear Physics. He discovered the atomic nucleus and explained how some elements naturally break down, for which he won the 1908 Nobel Prize in Chemistry. In 1911, he proposed the nuclear model of the atom. His portrait now appears on New Zealand’s $100 banknote."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 14,
          "bbox": [
            0.11,
            0.544,
            0.389,
            0.562
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "C. Discovery of the proton"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 15,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.565,
            0.776,
            0.617
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Rutherford showed that the nucleus carries positive charge which comes from the particles called "
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "protons.",
              "styles": [
                "bold"
              ]
            },
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": " Protons are much heavier than electrons and possess a charge equal and opposite to that of electrons."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 16,
          "bbox": [
            0.108,
            0.62,
            0.777,
            0.722
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "For an atom to be electrically neutral, the number of protons must be equal to the number of electrons. For example, a helium atom has 2 protons and 2 electrons, while a sodium atom has 11 protons and 11 electrons. Since the total positive charge equals the total negative charge, the helium or the sodium atoms are also electrically neutral. Similarly, all the atoms are electrically neutral."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "image",
          "index": 17,
          "bbox": [
            0.088,
            0.733,
            0.151,
            0.77
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "image_body",
              "index": 17,
              "bbox": [
                0.088,
                0.733,
                0.151,
                0.77
              ],
              "image_path": "images/page_5_image_17.jpg",
              "content": ""
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "index": 18,
          "bbox": [
            0.155,
            0.734,
            0.345,
            0.752
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Pause and Ponder"
            }
          ],
          "level": 2
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 19,
          "bbox": [
            0.153,
            0.757,
            0.876,
            0.79
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "7. Assertion (A): Rutherford concluded that most of the mass of an atom is concentrated in a small region at the centre called the nucleus."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 20,
          "bbox": [
            0.173,
            0.792,
            0.844,
            0.824
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Reason (R): According to Thomson’s model, electrons are embedded in a uniformly distributed positive charge sphere."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 21,
          "bbox": [
            0.175,
            0.825,
            0.393,
            0.84
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "Choose the correct option:"
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 22,
          "bbox": [
            0.178,
            0.84,
            0.694,
            0.856
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(i) Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 23,
          "bbox": [
            0.173,
            0.856,
            0.719,
            0.872
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(ii) Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 24,
          "bbox": [
            0.17,
            0.872,
            0.397,
            0.887
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(iii) A is true, but R is false."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "index": 25,
          "bbox": [
            0.17,
            0.887,
            0.397,
            0.903
          ],
          "content": [
            {
              "type": "text",
              "content": "(iv) A is false, but R is true."
            }
          ]
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
